# Sub-tick interpolation of the tracer ribbon and mesh
# (tests/harness/check_feeder_subtick.sh --path tracer).
#   <bk2> | <V> | blob=<x>,<y> ... [tol=<px>] [step=<px>]
#
# The two legs are a render-path selection with and without the tracer path,
# terrain and moby in both.
#
# The source is not a movie: the plain boot's attract demo, the presentation
# corpus's attract-demo row, whose pin 14573 reads tracers=30 on the
# intermediate present. Four live ribbon records draw 10 segments and one mesh
# descriptor 5 quads per tick over VBL 14563-14583. A tick spans two VBLs
# starting at an odd VBL here, so V=14575 and the intermediate present at
# 14576 covers [14573, 14575]. The ribbon at (471.9,201.3) crosses 9.4 px over
# the interval. The component at (344.6,219.3) is the mesh: two rings of five
# vertices, the leading ring moving about 350 world units per tick, so it
# crosses 2.9 px while its area ramps 780 to 1021 px, which is why the row's
# step floor is 2. Its vertices move toward the camera, so the projection of
# the lerped world vertices is not the screen lerp the cells compare against:
# its centroid and area cells read PARTIAL (0.40 px, 38.5 px) inside `tol`.
attract-demo | 14575 | blob=471.9,201.3 blob=344.6,219.3 tol=1.0 step=2
#
# Crush's fireball beam in games/spyro-2/bk2/crushs-dungeon.bk2: the indexed
# mesh, one 27.4-27.5k px component from the left screen edge to Crush. Its
# near end reaches in front of the near floor (four quads with a corner under
# view-Z 256 per tick around V). A build that left those quads at the tick's
# own corners on a sub-tick present doubled the near end into a flap at alpha
# 0 (centroid 9.67 px off canon(V-2), area 24729 px against 27500). The
# centroid moves 1.62 px over the interval, so the step floor is 1 and alpha
# 0 is judged: the flap is an alpha-0 displacement, which the default
# judged alphas (0.5, 1) do not read.
crushs-dungeon.bk2 | 3341 | blob=81.9,233.9 tol=1.0 step=1 judge=0,0.5,1
